ty, stress resilience, weather assessment (technical), computer skills. Concerns: memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication). So mixed.\n\nC: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Strengths: decision quality, calm under pressure, technical competence. Concerns: communication, egocentric, not willing to develop (adaptability). So strong technical and stress but poor cooperation.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, has a hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, concentration, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: know-it-all (cooperation), hot temper (stress resilience, crew cooperation). Mixed.\n\nWe need choose best overall fit. Likely B has reliability and stress but has nasty remarks. C has good technical and calm but poor communication. D has responsibility but hot temper. A has anticipation and leadership but not open to ideas.\n\nWhich candidate balances? B's nasty remarks may be serious for crew cooperation. C's egocentric and communication difficulty also serious. D's hot temper also serious. A's show-off and not open to ideas also concerning but maybe less severe? A's strengths are strong for long-distance: anticipation, spatial vision, leadership. Might be best overall if we can manage attitude. But we need evidence.\n\nWe can make recommendation for Candidate A, citing strengths and noting concerns but perhaps less severe than others. Provide argument.\n\nWe must also note that we haven't discussed others yet, but we can still give recommendation now, but per process we should cover all candidates before final. However we can still state provisional recommendation.\n\nThus: \"I propose Candidate A\". Provide evidence: anticipation, comp